Reason:
The bug occurs because `xa_get_order` is called without any lock protection, and the retrieved order value can become stale if a concurrent swap entry split or other modification changes the order before the subsequent `xa_cmpxchg_irq`. This can cause `free_swap_and_cache_nr` to free a wrong number of pages (e.g., based on an incorrect large order), leading to data corruption, hangs, or kernel panics. Additionally, the order could grow large and cause truncation to erase data beyond the intended end border.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
xas_lock_irq(&xas);
entry = xas_load(&xas);
if (entry == radswap) {
    nr_pages = 1 << xas_get_order(&xas);
    base = round_down(xas.xa_index, nr_pages);
    if (base < index || base + nr_pages - 1 > end)
        nr_pages = 0;
    else
        xas_store(&xas, NULL);
}
xas_unlock_irq(&xas);
if (nr_pages)
    free_swap_and_cache_nr(radix_to_swp_entry(radswap), nr_pages);
return nr_pages;
Reason:
The fix opens the Xarray cmpxchg operation using `xas_lock_irq`/`xas_unlock_irq` and retrieves the order with `xas_get_order` while still holding the lock, ensuring a consistent view of the entry. It also validates that the full swap entry range (from `base` to `base + nr_pages - 1`) does not exceed the `end` boundary; if it does, the entry is skipped (nr_pages set to 0) because the caller (`shmem_undo_range`) already handled partial truncation in a previous loop. This prevents the race and ensures correct freeing of swap entries.

